# Licensing

*School Boards and the Democratic Promise* — Jared E. Knowles, PhD dissertation,
University of Wisconsin–Madison, 2015.

Copyright © 2015 Jared E. Knowles, except where noted below.

A dissertation repository is not one work under one licence. It is scholarship,
software, public records, and other people's LaTeX, sitting in one directory
tree. Each is licensed on its own terms.

| What | Where | Licence |
|---|---|---|
| **Scholarship** — the dissertation text, figures, and the deposited PDF | `chapters/`, `frontmatter/`, `backmatter/`, `appendix/`, `img/`, `phd_figs/`, `dissertation.pdf`, `README.md`, `reference/2015-build/` | [CC BY 4.0](LICENSES/CC-BY-4.0.txt) |
| **Software** — analysis code and build infrastructure | `R/`, `data/*.R`, `scripts/`, `docker/*.R`, `docker/Dockerfile*`, `docker/entrypoint.sh`, `Makefile`, `build.R`, and the code chunks inside `chapters/*/*.Rnw` | [MIT](#mit-license) |
| **Data** — election returns and administrative records | `vendor/Data/`, `data/cache/`, `Wisconsin_SB_election_export_06042017.*` | Public records; see [Data](#data) |
| **Third-party** | `includes/`, `bib/*.bst`, `docker/vendor/` | Unchanged, under their own terms; see [Third-party](#third-party-components) |

If you use the research, cite the dissertation. If you use the code, the MIT
notice is enough. Attribution for CC BY 4.0 is satisfied by citing:

> Knowles, Jared E. (2015). *School Boards and the Democratic Promise.*
> PhD dissertation, University of Wisconsin–Madison.

## Why these licences

**CC BY 4.0 for the scholarship.** It is the norm for openly licensed academic
work and it lets others quote, translate, teach from, and build on the text with
nothing more than a citation — which is what scholarship is for. The author
retains copyright in the dissertation; the university deposit and ProQuest hold
only non-exclusive distribution rights, so this licence is the author's to grant.
No non-commercial clause: `NC` reliably blocks legitimate reuse — course packs,
Wikipedia, open textbooks, replication in a commercially funded lab — while doing
nothing to stop the enclosure it is imagined to prevent.

**MIT for the code.** The point of publishing analysis code is that someone else
can run it, lift a function out of it, and not have to think about the licence.

**Public records for the data**, because that is what they are.

## Data

The election returns, board rosters, and administrative records are **public
records** obtained from Wisconsin county clerks, the Wisconsin Department of
Public Instruction, the Wisconsin Employment Relations Commission, the U.S.
Census, and the National Center for Education Statistics. Facts are not
copyrightable in the United States.

To the extent any compilation copyright or database right subsists in the way
these records were assembled here, the author waives it under
[CC0 1.0](LICENSES/CC0-1.0.txt). Use them freely.

**On the named individuals.** `data/cache/SchoolBoardRosterVerification.csv` and
`data/cache/districtadmin0211.csv` contain names, and in the roster's case
mailing addresses, of elected school board members and appointed district
administrators. These are public officials and the records are public rosters and
election returns, published as such. See `vendor/README.md` and the data
collection appendix for provenance. The waiver above concerns copyright only and
is not a licence to use personal information in ways the law otherwise restricts.

## Third-party components

None of these are the author's work and none are relicensed here.

**UW–Madison thesis template** — `includes/thesisdefs.tex`,
`includes/preamble.tex`, `includes/mcbride.bst`, `includes/README`.
Copyright © 2008–2009 William C. Benton, under the **LaTeX Project Public
License**; the licence text is at `includes/LICENSE`. `mcbride.bst` derives from
`chicago2.bst` by Chung-chieh Shan, also LPPL. Any errors introduced by
modification here are the author's alone.

**Bibliography styles** — `includes/chicago.bst` derives from `chicago.bst`
v4 (1992) by Glenn Paulley, University of Waterloo, modified here to add the
`wb-epi` environment and change `citeN` to `cite`. `bib/apsa-leeper.bst` derives
from the Harvard-family `apsr.bst`. Both carry the standard BibTeX-style
condition that modified copies be renamed, which the filenames respect.

**Vendored R packages** — `docker/vendor/*.tar.gz` are **unmodified source
tarballs** redistributed from the CRAN Archive so the 2015 environment can be
rebuilt without depending on a decade-old repository staying online. Each carries
its own licence inside its `DESCRIPTION` and `LICENSE` files: predominantly
GPL-2 and GPL-3, with MIT, LGPL, Apache-2.0, BSD-3-Clause and BSL-1.0 among them.
`inst/pins/packages-2015.tsv` lists every package, version and checksum.

Redistributing unmodified GPL source with its licence intact is exactly what the
GPL permits, and placing those tarballs alongside this repository's own code is
mere aggregation — it does not make this project a derivative work of them, and
does not extend the GPL to the code licensed MIT above.

**NLopt** — `docker/vendor/nlopt-2.4.2.tar.gz` is the unmodified upstream
release by Steven G. Johnson, under LGPL-2.1-or-later with portions under MIT.

**Base image** — the container builds `FROM rocker/r-ver:3.1.2`, which is not
redistributed here.

